Keep the tracer and its syscall-heavy tracees on one CPU

Every intercepted syscall is a round trip between tracee and tracer. On
different CPUs each half wakes an idle CPU, and phones power idle CPUs down
deeply: 455 us to wake a Pixel 9a big core, so an intercepted stat() took
~250 us there and a getcwd() ~150 us. On one CPU the stop hands the CPU
straight to the tracer and back: ~30 us.

The tracer pins itself to the CPU with the highest capacity, and a tracee
that stops twice in a row after runs under 1 ms joins it. A pinned tracee
goes back to every CPU after two long runs in a row, or when it hasn't
stopped between two 20 ms checks, whether it computes or waits, so that
worker threads don't pile up on the tracer's CPU when they wake. The
checks only run while a pinned tracee is busy, so an idle shell doesn't
wake the tracer.

The guest doesn't see the pinning: sched_getaffinity() of a pinned thread
reports the device's CPUs (nproc, make -j, thread pools), and a thread
whose affinity the guest set itself is left alone, as are its children.

Android resets the affinities of an app's processes when it moves them
between cpusets (background/foreground). Every 250 ms the tracer checks
that it and the tracees it believes pinned are still pinned, and pins
again; while its CPU isn't available it pins nothing. Releasing a tracee
asks for every CPU of the device and the kernel limits that to its
current cpuset, so a proot started in the background isn't kept to the
CPUs it had then.

PROOT_HOME_CPU=<n> picks the CPU, PROOT_HOME_CPU=off disables it.

